    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 473,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 386,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62064,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 74,
        "headingId": 386,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Tumours are usually small (average size: 2&nbsp;cm). The cut surface is typically brown, red, or pink and typically shows a solid, well-circumscribed, lobulated, soft appearance {2553628}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:09:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 467,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 380,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62065,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 74,
        "headingId": 380,
        "headingText": "<p>These tumours typically comprise a well-circumscribed nodular growth of large polyhedral or rounded epithelioid cells with abundant eosinophilic, but occasionally pale, cytoplasm. Cytoplasmic lipochrome pigment is commonly seen. Clustering of nuclei, creating intervening eosinophilic nuclear-free zones, is a characteristic feature. Cytoplasmic Reinke crystals (rod-shaped elongated eosinophilic crystals) are often seen but may be rare or absent. The diagnosis is appropriate for tumours that lack identifiable Reinke crystals but show otherwise-typical features and are located in the ovarian hilus. Nuclei are typically round, with a single prominent nucleolus; nuclear pseudoinclusions may be present, and bizarre nuclear atypia is sometimes seen. Mitoses are rare or absent. In one&nbsp;third of cases, fibrinoid material is seen within blood vessel walls. Occasional tumours have a conspicuous fibrous stroma. Hyperplasia of non-neoplastic hilus/Leydig cells is commonly present in the uninvolved ovarian hilus {2553628}. Immunohistochemically, Leydig cell tumours are usually positive for sex cord&ndash;stromal markers, such as inhibin {9688187}, calretinin {11603218}, and melan-A {20567142}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-07-02T19:05:15"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 468,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 381,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62066,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 74,
        "headingId": 381,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:09:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
